 "answer": "I can't get a live reading, and the sources I found disagree because they were captured at different times and use different methods. Penang's air is probably in the moderate range, but it may be worse in some areas.\n\n| Source | Location | AQI | Category | Main pollutant |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Weather Underground (Copernicus data) | Penang | 68 | Moderate | PM2.5 |\n| IQAir | Bukit Mertajam | 58 | Moderate | PM2.5 (12.9 µg/m³) |\n| IQAir | George Town | 92 | Moderate | PM2.5 (31 µg/m³) |\n| AccuWeather | Penang airport | 67 | Poor | – |\n| AccuWeather | George Town | 153 | Very Unhealthy | – |\n| HazeMonitor | Minden (highest of 4 DOE stations) | 155 | Unhealthy | PM2.5 |\n\n- **Differences between sources:** The AccuWeather and HazeMonitor pages looked like older snapshots. Some of the dates shown on the pages were different, for example 9 Oct, 13 Oct and 15 Oct. Because of that, I can't tell which figure is current.\n- **Differences between places:** Readings also vary a lot by location and time of day. Town and traffic areas such as George Town and Minden tend to read higher than Bukit Mertajam.\n\n**What to do:**\n- For the current number, check IQAir's George Town page or the Malaysian Department of Environment's official API/IPU readings (the \"MyAQI\" site). The DOE readings are the official ones.\n- If the reading is above about 100, sensitive groups such as children, older people and people with asthma should limit prolonged outdoor exertion.\n\nIf you tell me which part of Penang you're in, I can search for that area.",
